  message: "SEQUENCED 2026-09-06: appended to the arc tail behind INV-busis (owner: \"everything behind the arc is append to the end of the arc\"), together with INV-varil (one PR: the three-tier gate, the twelve-item cleanup, --strict in CI, one config). Placed BEFORE the WI-vafad sweep in the tail so the sweep's many filings land under the gate."  # 8b57
- op: discuss  # 9cb2
  at: '2026-09-11T10:57:31Z'  # 9cb2
  by: agent  # 9cb2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9cb2
  clock: 6  # 9cb2
  nonce: 9cb2  # 9cb2
  message: "LAYER 1 IS CLOSED AND THE OTHER TWO ARE NOT, WITH THE REASON MEASURED RATHER\nTHAN GUESSED. Arc T4, PR pending. Design: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/t4_custom_key_gate_09112026/DESIGN.md\n\nWHAT SHIPPED — the write path (this item's layer 1).\n\n`hypergumbo_tracker/field_keys.py` + gates in `Store.add` and `Store.update`.\nThree tiers keyed on NORMALISATION (case, and `-`/`_`/space folded):\n\n  TIER 1  a CORE attribute name -> REFUSED, no --force, message names the flag\n          that actually moves it (\"use --status instead\").\n  TIER 2  normalises to a DECLARED schema key but spelled differently\n          (`Root_Cause`, `root-cause`) -> REFUSED; `--force-field-key` opens\n          this tier and ONLY this tier.\n  TIER 3  undeclared and distinct -> ALLOWED.\n\nIN THE STORE, NOT THE CLI, and that is the load-bearing choice: this item's own\nlayer-1 finding is that `Store.update` runs no schema check at all and its\nfield-name loop validates the TOP-LEVEL keys while never looking inside\n`set_fields[\"fields\"]` — which is exactly where a custom key goes. A CLI-only\ngate is bypassed by every other writer.\n\nA REMOVAL IS EXEMPT, deliberately: `--remove-field status` sets the value to\nNone, and a gate that refused it would freeze the corpus items in their broken\nstate permanently. Deleting the offending key IS the remedy.\n\nTHE CORPUS, RE-MEASURED TODAY rather than restated from this item's 2026-09-06\ntable (2,233 items): **11 items, 18 field instances** carry a core-name shadow,\nnot the 21 instances recorded then. The arc's \"12-item cleanup\" is 11 today.\nAll of them CLEANED, one decision at a time and not in a batch:\n\n  INV-dabov  fields.status='violated' under status=satisfied; priority/tags\n             duplicated the core values -> all three removed.\n  INV-vaduk  fields.status='todo_soft' priority='P3' under satisfied/P1 --\n             and `todo_soft` is not a legal invariant status -> removed.\n  INV-toguh  shadow tags carried `dead_code`, which the CORE tags LACKED ->\n             `dead_code` added to the core tags FIRST, then the shadows removed.\n  INV-muhij  **the core description was EMPTY** and the whole 3,284-character\n             description lived in `fields.description`. Deleting the shadow\n             would have destroyed the item's only description, so the content\n             was MOVED UP and then the shadow removed. This is why the design\n             said per-item decisions: a batch delete would have lost it.\n\nWHY `--strict` IS **NOT** IN CI, AND IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Cleaning the items\ndoes not clean the ops. `validation._validate_fields_schema(fname, idx, fields,\nschema, result)` takes an OP INDEX and validates that op's own payload, so the\nunit is the OP and not the compiled ITEM. All nine core-name errors are `op 0` —\nthe CREATE op — on items whose compiled state is now clean. After the cleanup\n`tracker validate --strict` still reports the same 26 errors, because a create\nop written in June is immutable history.\n\nSo turning `--strict` on in the three CI files\n(`.github/workflows/tracker-ci.yml:67`, `ci.yml:1290`,\n`.woodpecker/woodpecker.yml:320`) requires first deciding what the validator's\nUNIT should be. That is a design decision, it is this item's layer 2/3 rather\nthan layer 1, and it is filed rather than guessed at.\n\nTWO LIMBS NEED THE HUMAN, not another agent pass:\n  * `severity` (17 invariants, a real convention no schema names) should be\n    DECLARED in the invariant `fields_schema`, the same route `dogfood_anon_id`\n    took. `.agent/tracker/config.yaml` is mode `-r--rw-r--` and is **NOT\n    WRITABLE BY THE AGENT** even though the agent owns it. That is an explicit\n    control and was not worked around.\n  * the stale `.agent/tracker-workspace/config.yaml` is gitignored, untracked,\n    read by NOTHING (`TrackerSet` passes the canonical config object to all\n    three tier Stores), and DANGEROUSLY out of date: it still lists `holding`\n    as an allowed invariant status and omits `satisfied` and\n    `pending_validation` entirely. Deleting it is the arc's \"one config\" limb;\n    the durable half is `setup.py` / `configure.py`, which still create and\n    offer to update it.\n\nSTILL OPEN ON THIS ITEM AFTER THIS CHANGE: `work_item` has NO `fields_schema`,\nso the validator reports nothing for it at all — 42 undeclared keys\n(`dogfood_anon_id` 20, `statement` 13, `root_cause` 5, `rationale` 4) are\ninvisible to `validate` in any mode. The new write-path gate DOES cover them\nfor tiers 1 and 2; tier 2 simply cannot fire for a kind that declares nothing."  # 9cb2
- op: discuss  # 3d13
  at: '2026-09-11T17:48:39Z'  # 3d13
  by: agent  # 3d13
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3d13
  clock: 7  # 3d13
  nonce: 3d13  # 3d13
  message: "--strict IS NOW REACHABLE AND PASSES. PR #930 merged 2026-09-11.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY BLOCKING IT -- not what the arc recorded. The standing claim (from me, twice) was that --strict was blocked permanently by immutable history: four items carry core attributes inside op 0 fields, ops are append-only, so the nine errors could never be cleared. THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG. The fields-schema check ran from _validate_create_values with an OP INDEX, so it re-reported op 0 for the life of the item -- including fields that a later --remove-field op had ALREADY DELETED. All four repairs had landed months earlier. Every one of the 26 live warnings named a defect that no longer existed in the compiled item.\n\nTHE FIX IS THE SECOND HALF OF A MIGRATION THAT ALREADY HAPPENED. Commit f148a13a22 (2026-02-23) moved the allowed_statuses check from per-op to compiled-state for exactly this reason -- its message reads \"historical ops may have used statuses that were valid when written but are now restricted by per-kind allowed_statuses\". compile_ops was already being called in validate_ops_file. The fields half simply never came along. _validate_fields_schema now takes no op index and runs against compiled.fields beside the status check.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT LOOSEN. compile_ops applies per-key last-write-wins, so remove-then-re-add is STILL flagged -- \"some op removed it\" is not repair, the last write decides; there is a regression test for exactly that. And the required-field check gets STRICTER: a required field deleted by a later op used to pass because op 0 carried it, and now fails.\n\nTWO REAL DEFECTS SURFACED AND WERE REPAIRED, NOT SILENCED.\n(1) severity, on 17 invariants. OWNER RULING 2026-09-11: severity is a bad thing to normalize; priority is the only ranking axis, and if something is really a bigger deal it is a P1 rather than a more-urgent P2. Measured before the ruling: severity is NOT derivable from priority (priority 2 carried all three values -- 4 high, 6 medium, 3 low) so it was not a duplicate, but NOTHING READ IT -- 17 writers, zero consumers. Removed from all 17, not declared.\n(2) INV-hazov carried pr_ref in BOTH homes: the core attribute was null while fields[pr_ref] held 9e3a93a75b. This is precisely INV-varil tier 1, still live in compiled state. Deleting the field alone would have lost the reference, so the value was migrated to the core attribute first, then the field removed.\n\nSTATE NOW: live warnings 26 -> 0; scripts/tracker validate --strict exits 0 on dev; 2906 tracker tests pass; 100% coverage in isolation; mypy ratchet 669 delta +0.\n\nREMAINING FOR THIS ITEM, so it is not read as closed: the write path still ACCEPTS an undeclared key (tier 3 of the INV-varil gate is deliberately permissive), work_item still declares no fields_schema at all, and no CI workflow yet runs validate --strict. What changed is that --strict is now PASSABLE, which is the precondition for wiring it into CI. That wiring is the next step and has not been done."  # 3d13
